Instant-preview quality: lighting, soft edges and sharper floors, a level-camera guess for floors / ceilings / head-on walls, fewer tile seams; and (2026-11-05) the catalogue designs and doors, furniture and "other" objects in the instant preview.

FOR YOUR OWN SWATCHROOM PROJECT. The Terucon website does not edit its copy of swatchroom/ for this: it serves
the files this patch produces from site/overlays/studio/ instead, ahead of the studio's own (docs/DESIGN_STUDIO_ARCHITECTURE.md, section 12). This
patch is the same code, as a change to the 9 studio files it replaces, so you can take it into your project if you like the result. The 9 files are
the website's copy of your project's files made on 2026-10-28 (the sha256 of all of them are in site/overlays/studio/manifest.json); whether they are still
byte-identical to your original folder was last checked with diff -rq on 2026-11-05 for the first 8 of them (previewReason.js was not compared then).

  Changes (against the studio files as they are in the website's copy of swatchroom/):
    public/js/lib/fastrender/geometry.js                  shading clamp 0.35..1.65 -> 0.2..2.5; exposure exponent 0.35 -> 0.6, bounds 0.7..1.25 -> 0.45..1.5; the photo's light colour;
                                                          NEW 2026-11-05: tileSizeOf() (a finish may state previewTileWidthM / previewTileHeightM, which only the preview reads), textureCrop(),
                                                          isPhotographUrl() + flattenTone() (a photograph of a sheet, under /media/), a finish's own sheen in specularFor()
    public/js/lib/fastrender/provisional.js               the light read inside each surface (a guided filter on the surface's own mask); the level-camera guess for floors, ceilings and head-on walls;
                                                          NEW: the estimate also covers door, furniture and other (ESTIMATED_KINDS), with a plane standing on the floor at the object's foot
    public/js/lib/fastrender/renderer.js                  passes the light colour on (30% of it); repairs the seams of the catalogue's tile images on load;
                                                          NEW: crops a picture to the tile's proportions instead of stretching it, refuses the website's "picture not available" tile, flattens a photograph's light drift,
                                                          reads the tile size with tileSizeOf(), gives walls / floors / ceilings the GPU slots first
    public/js/lib/fastrender/previewReason.js             NEW: only the wording of "no-fit" (it now covers doors and furniture too)
    public/js/lib/fastrender/shaders/swap.glsl.js         soft edge on both sides of a boundary; light colour; highlight roll-off; the new clamp
    public/js/lib/fastrender/shaders/swap.wgsl.js         the same, for WebGPU
    public/js/lib/fastrender/webgl2.js                    the light colour uniform; 8x anisotropic filtering
    public/js/lib/fastrender/webgpu.js                    the light colour in the globals block; 8x anisotropic sampler
    public/js/lib/previewScene.js                         passes the light colour through for a fitted scene; NEW: borrows the estimate for every kind in ESTIMATED_KINDS

  Apply from the folder that CONTAINS your swatchroom/ folder (the paths start with swatchroom/):
      git apply --check docs/design-studio-checks/instant-preview-quality.patch      (nothing changes; says whether it fits)
      git apply docs/design-studio-checks/instant-preview-quality.patch
  (or: patch -p1 < the file). It applies only to the files as they were on that date: if your copy has moved on, merge by hand.

  Three of the studio's own tests assert the old behaviour on purpose and fail afterwards: tests/fastrender.test.js, "the shading multiplier ... [0.35, 1.65]",
  "exposureFor()" (0.7 / 1.25) and "buildProvisionalScene() ..." (which asserts "no sofa" and that "shading is smooth where the photo flickers" < 0.02 across the sofa's boundary, and
  that a wall is 2.6 m tall: the later assertions are hidden behind the first one that fails). The new expectations are in the website's tests/js/studio-overlay-behaviour.test.js,
  marked CHANGED. (The studio's own preview-reason test is not affected: it asserts the reason codes, not the wording.)
  The catalogue designs themselves are NOT in this patch: they are added to the studio's finish list by the website (site/catalogue-ui/studio-finishes.js), and the AI render
  gets their photograph through server/lib/studioReference.js. A finish that states previewTileWidthM / previewTileHeightM and a texture.url draws in the preview with this patch; without it, it does not.
  The comments in the code that start with "DRAFT:" mark the earlier changes; rename or drop them when you adopt it.

  Measured, not guaranteed: tests and a real-browser check of WebGL2 and WebGPU on one computer (an Apple M5): docs/design-studio-checks/render-preview/ (run-all.mjs, design-check.mjs).
  Not verified: a real fused fit from the live room analysis (the level-camera guess was only tried on the estimate path), other GPUs, phones.

